        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"llm-gemini 0.33 released with Gemini 3.7 Flash support\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"Plugin adds support for Gemini 3.7 Flash plus gemini-3.6-flash, gemini-3.5-flash-lite and two embedding models. Upgraded for LLM 0.32 compatibility, enabling visibility of reasoning traces and server-side tools. Simon tested different thinking effort levels (high, medium) - high produced excellent pelican-on-bicycle artwork.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-13\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\n    10→      \"gemini\",\n    11→      \"llm\",\n    12→      \"llm-gemini\",\n    13→      \"gemini-3.7-flash\",\n    14→      \"reasoning-traces\",\n    15→      \"tool-use\"\n    16→    ],\n    17→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    18→  },\n    19→  {\n    20→    \"title\": \"sqlite-utils 4.2.1 released fixing crash from missing dependency\",\n    21→    \"description\": \"Quick fix for 4.2 which had a crashing bug due to typing-extensions not being listed as dependency. Simon figured out how to run smoke tests with `uv run --isolated --no-default-groups` to ensure CLI works without dev dependencies. Demonstrates importance of testing isolated installs.\",\n    22→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    23→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    24→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    25→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-13\",\n    26→    \"topics\": [\n    27→      \"sqlite-utils\",\n    28→      \"python\",\n    29→      \"packaging\",\n    30→      \"uv\",\n    31→      \"dependencies\",\n    32→      \"testing\"\n    33→    ],\n    34→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    35→  },\n    36→  {\n    37→    \"title\": \"DeepSeek V4 Pro 0813 released on OpenRouter\",\n    38→    \"description\": \"DeepSeek V4 Pro 0813 model released with 1.7T parameters (893 GB). Simon tested different reasoning levels (low, medium, high) and got very different pelican illustrations - more variation than any other model. Benchmarks reportedly released to WeChat group then copied to Reddit (deleted for 'low-effort') then to Hacker News.\",\n    39→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    40→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    41→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    42→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n    43→    \"topics\": [\n    44→      \"deepseek\",\n    45→      \"deepseek-v4-pro\",\n    46→      \"llm-release\",\n    47→      \"open-weights\",\n    48→      \"benchmarks\",\n    49→      \"reasoning-models\"\n    50→    ],\n    51→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    52→  },\n    53→  {\n    54→    \"title\": \"Alchemy-utils 0.1a0: Database-agnostic sqlite-utils prototype built by AI\",\n    55→    \"description\": \"Simon tasked Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol Ultra with building a database-agnostic version of sqlite-utils using SQLAlchemy. The AI produced a working prototype that can query PostgreSQL, SQLite, and DuckDB. Simon used a one-liner to query his blog's PostgreSQL database and another to import 100K SF trees into DuckDB (optimized from 1 hour to 35 seconds with AI help).\",\n    56→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    57→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    58→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    59→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n    60→    \"topics\": [\n    61→      \"sqlite-utils\",\n    62→      \"sqlalchemy\",\n    63→      \"codex\",\n    64→      \"gpt-5.6\",\n    65→      \"coding-agents\",\n    66→      \"duckdb\",\n    67→      \"postgresql\",\n    68→      \"python\"\n    69→    ],\n    70→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"Research paper shows encrypted chain-of-thought blocks from Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google could be extracted by replaying traces into weaker sibling models and jailbreaking them. The authors found every model in the same family used the same encryption key. Claude Haiku 4.5 was easiest to attack. All providers have now fixed this vulnerability.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-11\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\n    80→      \"reasoning-traces\",\n    81→      \"jailbreaking\",\n    82→      \"security\",\n    83→      \"openai\",\n    84→      \"anthropic\",\n    85→      \"google\",\n    86→      \"encrypted-thoughts\",\n    87→      \"llm-security\"\n    88→    ],\n    89→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    90→  },\n    91→  {\n    92→    \"title\": \"There are no lossless transformations of natural-language text\",\n    93→    \"description\": \"Sophie Alpert shares policy on AI writing by engineers: 'You must stand behind every idea and every sentence in your docs.' Every rewrite changes meaning, and if done by an entity without your detailed mental representation, information will be lost. Critical insight for using LLMs in technical writing.\",\n    94→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    95→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    96→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    97→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-11\",\n    98→    \"topics\": [\n    99→      \"ai-writing\",\n   100→      \"documentation\",\n   101→      \"lossless-transformations\",\n   102→      \"llms\",\n   103→      \"technical-writing\"\n   104→    ],\n   105→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   106→  },\n   107→  {\n   108→    \"title\": \"Introducing Muse Glimmer: Meta's new 30B Apache 2.0 model\",\n   109→    \"description\": \"Meta released Muse Glimmer, a 30B model under Apache 2.0 license optimized for end-to-end agentic task completion with strong benchmarks on DeepSearch QA, MCP-Atlas, SWE-Bench. Reliable tool use, multi-step reasoning. Simon tested with llm-coding-agent plugin and vision capabilities - works well with 32GB+ RAM.\",\n   110→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   111→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   112→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   113→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-10\",\n   114→    \"topics\": [\n   115→      \"meta\",\n   116→      \"muse-glimmer\",\n   117→      \"open-weights\",\n   118→      \"local-llms\",\n   119→      \"tool-use\",\n   120→      \"coding-agents\",\n   121→      \"vision-llms\"\n   122→    ],\n   123→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   124→  },\n   125→  {\n   126→    \"title\": \"GitHub Models is now retired\",\n   127→    \"description\": \"GitHub Models has been retired. Simon suspects the pattern where coding agent patterns made free/subsidized tokens prohibitively expensive led to shutdown. He switched to using OpenAI API key with spending limit for his repository README summaries using GPT-5.6 Luna.\",\n   128→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   129→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   130→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   131→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-09\",\n   132→    \"topics\": [\n   133→      \"github\",\n   134→      \"github-models\",\n   135→      \"github-actions\",\n   136→      \"llm-pricing\",\n   137→      \"coding-agents\"\n   138→    ],\n   139→    \"type\": \"news\"\n   140→  },\n   141→  {\n   142→    \"title\": \"SQLite compressed text-history prototypes\",\n   143→    \"description\": \"Simon had an idea for storing revision histories in SQLite by compressing all versions as JSON array with zlib/zstd. Discussed prototype with GPT-Live voice mode, then had GPT-5.6 Sol Pro build it. 1,000 revisions (20.4 MB raw) compressed to 80.3 KB. Suggested breaking history into chunks of 128 revisions or 3MB to optimize decompression.\",\n   144→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   145→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   146→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   147→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-09\",\n   148→    \"topics\": [\n   149→      \"sqlite\",\n   150→      \"compression\",\n   151→      \"gpt-5.6\",\n   152→      \"prototyping\",\n   153→      \"voice-mode\",\n   154→      \"zstd\"\n   155→    ],\n   156→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   157→  },\n   158→  {\n   159→    \"title\": \"Auto mode is now the default in Claude Code for Pro, Max, and Team plans\",\n   160→    \"description\": \"Anthropic is making auto mode the default for Claude Code starting August 14th. They report that in tests, only 13.6% of humans refused harmful actions, while auto mode would have blocked 89% of those actions. Third-party evaluation from Trajectory Labs tested 720 attack attempts against Claude with auto mode and found none succeeded. Simon notes he's predicted 'a challenger disaster for coding agents security' for 2026 and would like to be proved wrong.\",\n   161→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   162→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   163→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   164→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-08\",\n   165→    \"topics\": [\n   166→      \"claude-code\",\n   167→      \"anthropic\",\n   168→      \"security\",\n   169→      \"auto-mode\",\n   170→      \"coding-agents\",\n   171→      \"prompt-injection\",\n   172→      \"evals\"\n   173→    ],\n   174→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   175→  }\n   176→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
